+- 2026-10-09 — On review of #631: ICE lives inline in its node's grounding doc (spine v2), never as a separate file; a capability may exist before its domain (`/specify-domains` attaches it); an ICE is workable only when built from a confirmed business intent (spine `intents`) — unlinked ICE is kept, not worked on, never blocked.
+- 2026-10-17 — v4 (#637): one product model that every play writes, at the depth it knows, and nothing waits for another play. ICE now has one named place — `## Agent intent (ICE)`, the last section of a capability's or functionality's page, in labelled lines filled as far as is known; expected once the node names a business intent (missing is a linter warning, so earlier pages still pass). A business intent's touches are kept on its page as a **Touches** list (adds / changes / removes, with links), and the domain's spine entry gains `intents`, so every part an intent touches is linked both ways. A domain page is light (directional) or full (detailed), like a capability's. Design: `.garura/project/issues/637/specs/design.md`.
+- 2026-10-18 — #656: personal names taken out of the Source example and of this Log, so the ontology can live in Phoenix memory for any project. No kind, field or rule changed.
+- 2026-10-21 — v5 (#597, plan #722, with the product owner): Capability, Functionality, Work and Plan defined. Capabilities and functionalities **need** each other in any mix, and record what they were **identified by**; business intents are identified by each other — a connection, never a dependency. Work is never linked. Domains link loosely to business intents and capabilities and are never in a plan. The ready rule drops the domain: a node is ready once it serves a confirmed business intent and nothing it needs is open (replaces the ADR 043 rule). Q7 added. The spine field stays `depends_on` for now; its rename to `needs` is #725. Working notes: `.garura/project/issues/621/specs/ontology-run.md`.
+- 2026-10-22 — v6 (#758, plan #756, with the product owner): the ICE becomes its own node, with its own page (`ice.md`, in its part's folder) and its own spine entry (a new `ices` list), one for each capability and each functionality, in three parts (Intent, Context, Expectation); it has no link to a business intent, and finds them through its part. A business intent holds no ICE and is never worked on directly; it has one or more capabilities, its Touches list names domains and capabilities only, it gains an optional Rules property, and business intents are still identified by one another, a connection and never a dependency. Among the parts of the product, "needs" and "identified by" join capabilities only; a functionality has neither, and takes its business intents and order of work from its one parent capability. Work carries one ICE. A capability sits in at most one domain and a domain has one or more capabilities; a missing domain is a gap, never a stop; the spine's single `domain` field and the folders stay as they are. The ready rule moves to the ICE, with three answers: ready, partly ready, not ready; an ICE starts only with all three parts present. Q1–Q7 now point at the new links; Q8–Q16 added. Garura's own fn-727 → fn-724 becomes cap-718 → cap-723 (#765). Option 1 (one ICE for each part) replaces option 1.B (one ICE for each pair of business intent and part).
+- 2026-10-22 — v7 (#793, plan #788, with the product owner): the model has two layers with the Plan as the thread, and each kind says what it is in the tracker as. Product layer: Business Intent (an issue labelled `product`, the only kind with that label), Source, Domain, Capability, ICE, Feature (Functionality renamed; in the tracker represented by a Story) and the new Slice (an ordered group of features from any capabilities that gives one vertical a person can test; in the tracker represented by an Epic); Feature and Slice are not issue types; Source, Domain, Capability and ICE are model only, and Domain and Capability have no link to any work item. Work layer: Work is no longer a kind but the name of the layer, and Epic, Story, Spike, Chore and Bug are kinds, each an issue labelled `work-item`; Feature is no longer a work type. A Plan (a file) is given one business intent, picks a slice by two tests and creates one epic for it, or uses the one that exists; an Epic represents one slice, holds stories, serves one or more business intents, has no ICE, stays open and leaves the product model (the spine's `epics` list and the `epic.md` pages go); a Story represents and delivers one feature, one per feature in each epic, and its issue holds its scope of work, written from its feature's and capability's ICE, in place of the ICE copy; Spikes, Chores and Bugs are children of one story and carry no ICE — a Spike only after its story is built, with a new "led to" link, and a Bug under the story that introduced the defect. A capability is done when every feature in it has a closed story, worked out and never stored. The spine's slice entry keeps `id`, `slug`, `feature_refs` and `record`, gains `vertical`, and drops `domain_ref`, `status`, `order`, `effort` and `depends_on`. The spine and file names for features (`features`, `feature.md`, `features/`) come with the rename story next in plan 788. Domain becomes a full kind; Tracker Issue is not made a kind, its labels live on each kind. Q1, Q2, Q7, Q13 and Q16 change; Q17–Q26 are added. Dropped: Work as a kind, "one capability has one Feature", "a Feature carries its capability's ICE", "one functionality has exactly one Story", "Spikes, Bugs and Chores sit under a functionality", "each piece of work carries one ICE".
+- 2026-10-23 — v8 (#807, plan #788): wording only. The rename of functionality to feature is now done on disk, so the notes "until the rename story lands" go from the Feature, Slice and ICE kinds. Source gains its Layer line (product) and its In the tracker as line (none — model only), as every other kind has. The template gains both lines. No kind, link or rule changes meaning.
+- 2026-10-23 — v9 (#816, plan #788, with the product owner): a Slice serves one or more business intents (spine `intents`, the first being the one it was first shaped for), written by `/design-slice`, which writes nothing else (no plan, no epic, no story, no eval); the link is kept on the slice's side only, and the Business Intent is a destination that a slice serves. The rule "a slice has a screen or surface a person can test end to end" is the Slice's own. The Slice gains one eval and one usage guide, also when two plans hold it, both kept in the folder of its first plan (`.garura/project/plans/<plan-id>/evals/<slug>/` and `guides/<slug>.md`); another plan points to them and never copies them. The eval is built by the eval play, is never read by a builder, reads only the slice's epic, its stories and the usage guide, and tests what the epic delivers against the built product from the outside, never reading the ICE or the code; the guide is delivered by the build and is the eval's only way in. The Plan is made by the plan play, right after `/design-slice`, from the slice it shaped, or that slice is added to the one plan its business intent already has; it holds the slices that serve its intent, read from the link; it creates one epic for each slice, or uses the one that exists, and the stories of that epic; its folder keeps its slices' evals and guides. An Epic serves the intents its slice serves, is read by its slice's eval, and its title says what a person can see the product do. A Story is written by the plan play from its two ICE, as before; its scope of work names what the feature must do, what is expected and what must never happen, and its slice's eval reads it. The ICE does not change. Q1's answer, Q7 and Q25 change; Q27–Q31 are added. Dropped: "a plan picks a slice by two tests", "a slice with no surface fails the plan's first test, and is never picked".
+- 2026-10-23 — v10 (#826, plan #788, with the product owner): the routing table of `idd-principles.md` holds word for word. A Story holds the goal, the limits and the expectations of its two ICE in its scope of work, and the Context guidance (the four Context lines of its two ICE) as its own new property, and never a failure condition or a recovery condition. The Story does not hold the context itself: the build play's context sub-agent reads the guidance, collects the context at run time and puts it in the issue's short-term memory, so the builder gets all of Context and never opens an ICE page. The plan play writes each story's failure conditions, and exactly one recovery condition for each, into the slice's hidden file `.garura/project/plans/<plan-id>/evals/<slug>/failure-and-recovery.md`, from the same two ICE, so each ICE is interpreted once; the Plan's folder says so. The Slice's eval is read at two times: the eval play reads its epic, its stories and the hidden file, never the guide or the code; the eval, when it runs, reads the eval, the usage guide, the Context guidance in the eval and the recovery conditions. It tests that no failure condition in the hidden file happens, and when one does, it writes a handoff plan that says what the product does wrong and never names the condition; the plan goes to the builder or to a person, as the recovery condition says. The ICE does not change. Q26 and Q29 change; Q32–Q35 are added. Dropped: "a story's scope of work says what must never happen". Review fixes: the ICE rule names the plan play as its reader; the running eval reads only the recovery parts; the Story says 'limits'; the recovery rule in standards/rules/expectation-generation.md now covers a story's recovery.
+- 2026-10-24 — v11 (#846, plan #788, with the product owner): `/design-slice` writes the slice's hidden file, and the plan play writes only the plan file, the epic and the stories. Slice: `/design-slice` writes the link, thin ICE where a part has none, and the hidden file; the hidden file has one entry for each feature, not each story; `/design-slice` makes the eval folder before any plan file exists. Plan: the plan play writes only the plan file, the epic and the stories, nothing in the evals folder, and reads every ICE line except 'Fails if'. Epic: gains the feature-to-story map, written by the plan play and read by the eval play to join each hidden-file entry to its story. Story: its failure and recovery conditions are written by `/design-slice`; when an ICE changes, the plan play remakes the story and `/design-slice` remakes the entry, in the same Alignment round. ICE: `/design-slice` reads its 'Fails if' lines and the plan play reads the rest; an ICE can be thin (at least a goal, a limit and one 'Fails if' line in Intent, one line in Context and one in Expectation, with the thin mark on its page and in its spine entry), proposed by `/design-slice`, which warns the person and never sends them back, and the ready check then answers 'partly ready' with the gap 'thin ICE'; 'light ICE' becomes 'thin ICE'. The flow line, link line 1, Q29, Q33 and Q34 change; Q36–Q40 are added. Dropped: "the plan play writes the slice's hidden file", "the plan play reads all of each ICE", "a light ICE has one line in each of its three parts".
+- 2026-10-25 — v12 (#875, plan #788, with the product owner): an Epic is an issue of type Epic and a Story an issue of type Story, each with no label, in place of "an issue labelled `work-item`". The organization now has the Epic type, so an Epic is never filed as type Feature, and an epic found with another type is set to type Epic (this overtakes #818's decision 5). A story found with another type is set to type Story. A play finds an epic or a story by its plan file's `represents` mark, never by its type or its title. The opening line about the tracker says the label `product` is on a Business Intent only and the label `work-item` on a Spike, a Chore or a Bug only; those four kinds keep their labels in this change. Q20 becomes "Given a tracker issue, which kind is it?", with its new answer. No other kind changes.
+- 2026-10-29 — v13 (#717, plan #717, with the product owner): the Plan kind drops the rule "A plan that needs another cannot land on main until that plan is done: the other plan has landed on main, or has been merged into this plan's branch." When a plan's branch goes into main is a rule of delivery, which the harness standard (principle 8) and the work guide hold; ADR 060 decides that a plan's branch merges into main when the plan pauses, also with its issues still open. The property "Needs plans", the link "needs another plan" and the other rules stay. No question and no other kind changes.
+- 2026-10-30 — v14 (#720, plan #717, with the product owner): the Plan kind catches up with how plans are kept. A plan's Status is Not Started, In Progress, Paused, Done or Dropped (was active, done or dropped), and it has a Direction (1, 2, 3 or none); a person sets both, and nothing works them out. Its tree may hold six item kinds, each under one kind of parent: an epic at the top, a feature item under an epic, a story under an epic or a feature item, a spike, chore or bug under a story. A feature item is an issue of type Feature that groups stories inside an epic: the Plan's own word, not the Feature kind, and it represents nothing in the model; a story under it sits in its epic. While its work is in progress a plan has its own branch, `plan/<id>/base`. The plan file keeps no history: each landing is one commit whose message names what was asked, the overrides and what is owed. Managing a plan is `/reshape-plan`, which changes the plan file, the tracker and the product model together, on one approval. These stay in the Plan kind and the plan files, never in the product model. The ICE's Thin line becomes "Thin: proposed from the person's words; deepen with `/specify-capabilities`.", and a thin ICE is proposed by the play that needs it (today `/design-slice` or `/reshape-plan`); a page with the old line is still thin. The opening names the type Feature. Q41–Q47 are added; Q7, Q20 and Q38 change, and Q1, Q4, Q22 and Q23 name the feature item. No other kind changes.
+- 2026-10-31 — v15 (#1007, ADR 064, plan #717, with the product owner): an item's status in a plan follows its own issue. Closed as completed is Done; closed as not planned is Dropped; while the issue is open, a person sets it. There are still no roll-ups: a parent does not change because its children closed, and the plan's Status and Direction stay the person's. Only the Plan kind's status rule changes; no question, kind, link or property changes.
+- 2026-11-12 — v16 (#1050, ADR 068, with the product owner): each product feature has one Feature issue, a new work-layer kind: one issue of type Feature, with no label, whose text names the feature (`Represents: <feature id>`) — the only link, one way, from the tracker to the model; no feature holds an issue number. The Feature issue holds the scope of work and Context guidance (moved from the Story), sits under one Epic at a time (a second Epic asks the person), and holds the Stories; a Story always sits inside one, and an orphan Story is matched to its feature by research in the model. A play finds it by the plan file's mark, else by the exact line on an issue of type Feature, open or closed, never by title; two such issues stop the run. A feature is built when a person closes its Feature issue as completed through `/reshape-plan` "finish" (closed as not planned is not built), and then counts complete in every slice and plan that holds it; slices and plans group features many to many and take in only features not yet built. A plan whose epic does not hold a feature's Feature issue shows it with the mark `held_by: <epic>`; a move is made in a `/reshape-plan` session of the plan it moves to, whose commit names the plan it leaves as owed. The feature item goes, and "delivered" becomes "built" for a feature. Capability and ICE change in wording only. Changed kinds: Feature, Story, Epic, Plan, Slice, Capability, ICE; new kind: Feature issue. Q19, Q20, Q26 and Q43 change; Q1, Q4, Q7, Q10, Q13, Q18, Q22, Q23, Q29, Q32, Q36, Q37 and Q39 follow; Q48–Q53 are added. The opening, the flow line, the five links and the tracker paragraph change.
+- 2026-11-12 — v17 (#954, ADR 069, with the product owner): the Plan is the one place where the product model meets the work, and the model knows no plan, no tracker and no decision record — a new rule for the whole model: no part holds a reference to the work side (plan, plan folder, eval, hidden file, epic, tracker issue or the tracker), `created_by` and `updated_by` name the play or agent, never an issue, git history keeps where a part came from, the spine has no `epics` list and no work status, a product decision is the content of the part it decides, and an ADR records a decision about the code, outside the model. A plan names its business intent at the top of the plan file, has one Epic (the mark `epic: <issue number>`), which holds the Feature issues of all its slices and the plan's chores, records each slice in its Slices list with a status a person sets (Not Started, In Progress, Done or Dropped), and has one eval that covers every feature of its slices; a done plan lands on main only when that eval passes (by hand until #801), and a paused plan still merges; its folder keeps that eval, the hidden files of the slices first shaped for its intent and the usage guides; a new slice joins it through `/reshape-plan`. Epic: one for each plan; it stands for no slice; the shared epic and its `represents` mark go. Slice: names no plan, epic, eval, hidden file or guide, and is model only; its eval, hidden file and guide move to the plan. Business Intent: no Tracker line, and What was done links to no work item. Chore: under a story when the upkeep is for that story's work, else under the plan's epic. Feature issue: wording only. Product Profile is listed as not yet defined. Changed kinds: Plan, Epic, Slice, Business Intent, Chore, Feature issue. The opening, the flow line, links 1, 2 and 4 and the tracker paragraph change. Q1, Q7, Q18, Q20, Q28 to Q33, Q35, Q36, Q40 and Q43 change; Q54 to Q63 are added (Q62 partly).
